  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 130 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nFocus shifts to next week's rain chances. A deep longwave trough is \nforecast to be passing through the Desert Southwest Tuesday night. \nDespite the distance, model guidance shows the system ejecting lobes \nof vorticity into the Mid-Mississippi Valley during the same \ntimeframe. Concurrently, high pressure will dominate the low-level \nwind pattern, resulting in moisture and warm air advection into the \ncenter of the country. In these southeast Missouri and southwest \nIllinois the low-levels may saturate immediately or remain dry for \nmuch longer than to the northwest. Though ensembles show a 100% \nchance of rain between 06z Wednesday and 06z Friday everywhere, \nthese areas hold the most uncertainty regarding timing and amounts. \nElsewhere, and in northeast Missouri especially, moisture is \nexpected to pool along an approaching front with rain all but \nguaranteed (LREF 90-100% probability of 24 hour rainfall). The LREF \nprobability of 24 hour rainfall >0.50\" ending 06z Friday ranges from \n40%-80% from southeast to northwest. Even with the uncertainty, this \nsystem still brings optimism regarding the ongoing drought.\n\nTemperatures next week will be on the upswing as warm air is pulled \ninto the Mid-Mississippi Valley ahead of the aforementioned system. \nBy Tuesday, much of the area is forecast to reach the upper 70s to \n80 degrees. Confidence in temperatures drops come Wednesday due to \nuncertainties regarding the timing of the rain's onset as seen in \nthe 13 degrees NBM IQR spread at KSTL that day. The spread tightens \nsome in the following days, but this false confidence is dispelled \nwhen considering the continued rain chances and potential cold \nfrontal passage later in the week.",
